cellGcm: take a JUMP waiting at the idle get when the title stores put.

The RSX starts fetching the moment put moves away from get, and Resistance 3 relies on it. It runs each streamed
texture copy at the idle get G: a JUMP to the copy block at G, a JUMP back to G after the block, put = the block's
end; once get reaches that end it sets put back to G and reuses the block's memory shortly after. The walker only runs
on the present thread, which can be busy for tens of milliseconds (pipeline builds at a level start), so it often
resumed from the block's end after the reuse and read texture or game data as commands -- several FIFO derails per
run, each ending in an unmapped JUMP/CALL and a resync. One of those garbage words once set ZSTENCIL_CLEAR_VALUE to
a depth near the near plane: every later depth clear then culled the whole scene and the screen stayed flat yellow.

cellGcm_on_put_store now follows JUMPs at get on the storing thread (at most 8, never a park or an unmapped target),
under the walker's lock, and publishes get as the walker does. Methods are left to the walker, since they need the
backend on the present thread. A put store made by the walker itself (the ring recycle) skips it.

tests/gcm_put_jump drives that injection through the walker with no backend: get is back at G as soon as put is
stored, and the block's memory overwritten afterwards is never executed.

The put word's store reaches cellGcm_on_put_store from vm_write32 (ppu_loader.cpp).
The two loader fixtures that link ppu_loader.cpp (test_ppu_entry_arguments, test_loaded_image_memory) stub it, as they
stub the other subsystems the loader reaches.

Upstream: not proposed yet.

---

